🌳 Java Collection Deep Dive — TreeSet (Internal Working)
1. Introduction

TreeSet is a SortedSet implementation that stores elements in ascending order by default (or a custom order via Comparator).

Internally, it uses a NavigableMap, specifically a TreeMap, to store elements.

So:

TreeSet<E> = new TreeMap<E, Object>();


The value stored in the map is a dummy constant object, similar to HashSet.

2. Key Features
Feature	Description
Ordering	Sorted (natural or custom Comparator)
Duplicates	Not allowed
Nulls	Not allowed (throws NullPointerException)
Backed by	TreeMap (Red-Black Tree)
Thread-safe	❌ No
Allows heterogeneous objects	❌ No (throws ClassCastException)
3. Internal Data Structure

TreeSet internally uses a Red-Black Tree, a type of self-balancing binary search tree.

Every insertion or deletion ensures:

Tree remains balanced

Operations stay O(log n)

4. Source Code Simplified
public class TreeSet<E> extends AbstractSet<E>
        implements NavigableSet<E>, Cloneable, java.io.Serializable {

    private transient NavigableMap<E, Object> m;
    private static final Object PRESENT = new Object();

    public TreeSet() {
        this(new TreeMap<E, Object>());
    }

    public boolean add(E e) {
        return m.put(e, PRESENT) == null;
    }
}


So internally:

TreeSet<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();
set.add(10); // internally calls: treeMap.put(10, PRESENT);

5. Internal Working (Step-by-Step)

Let’s say:

TreeSet<Integer> ts = new TreeSet<>();
ts.add(40);
ts.add(20);
ts.add(60);
ts.add(10);
ts.add(30);


Internally, each add() → TreeMap.put(key, PRESENT)

TreeMap compares the new key with existing ones using compareTo().

Positions the new key in the Red-Black Tree structure.

Re-balances tree if needed.

Resulting structure:

        40
       /  \
     20    60
    / \
  10  30

6. Red-Black Tree Rules

To stay balanced, the Red-Black Tree maintains these properties:

Each node is either red or black.

Root is always black.

Red nodes cannot have red children.

Every path from a node to its descendant null nodes must have the same number of black nodes.

When you insert/delete:

Tree rebalances itself by performing rotations (left or right).

